{"status":"question","question":{"text":"Last thing before I recommend a stack. For the ONE thin vertical slice that proves the skeleton and the tests are genuinely wired together, my recommendation is: importing a single CSV row and showing it categorised on screen — it touches upload → parse → persist → render in one line, and a test can watch that whole path. I'd keep categorisation dead-simple for the slice (one hardcoded rule, e.g. contains \"COFFEE\" → \"Dining\"), with real category logic deferred. Does that slice work as the proof, or would you rather the foundation prove a different path (e.g. just persisting a manually-typed transaction)?","choices":["Import one CSV row and show it categorised","Manually add one transaction and show it","Something else"],"recommended":"Import one CSV row and show it categorised"},"prd":"# Mission: Personal finance website — foundation\n\n**Type:** feature\n**Goal:** Stand up a running skeleton of a shared budget & expense tracker with a wired test runner and one thin vertical slice proving they connect.\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- App boots and shows something\n- Test runner wired and watchable by a human\n- One thin slice exercises CSV import → categorise → display end-to-end, backed by the shared database\n**Out of Scope:**\n- Budgets, charts, bank sync, real categorisation rules, multi-user auth polish (deferred)\n**Constraints:**\n- Shared server-side database from the start (two users)\n- TypeScript full-stack (stack pending captain confirmation)\n"}

